1. File Permissions (chmod)
Linux permissions = r (4), w (2), x (1)
Example:

chmod 755 file.sh
Owner → 7 (rwx)
Group → 5 (r-x)
Others → 5 (r-x)
Linux uses a permission model based on read, write, execute. I usually use numeric notation like 755 or symbolic like chmod u+x. For example, scripts need execute permission to run.

2. Hard vs Soft Links

Hard links share the same inode, so even if the original file is deleted, data remains. Soft links are symbolic pointers and break if the original file is removed.

ln file.txt hardlink.txt
ln -s file.txt softlink.txt

3. Process Management
ps aux – Snapshot debugging
Scenario:
Production API is slow, but system is still responding.
ps aux | grep java

I use ps aux when I need a quick snapshot of running processes to identify abnormal resource usage

top / htop – Live monitoring
Scenario:
Server CPU suddenly spikes to 100%-80% 
top or htop
Why:

Real-time CPU, memory usage
Sort by CPU (Shift + P) or Memory (Shift + M)
Easier to spot problematic process instantly

Real example:

During deployment, one container started looping → CPU spike → detected via htop

Interview line:

“I use top/htop for real-time monitoring when there is a sudden spike in CPU or memory


kill -9 <pid> – Force kill stuck process

Scenario:
Application is stuck / not responding to normal kill

Steps:

kill <pid>      # Try graceful first (SIGTERM)
kill -9 <pid>   # Force kill (SIGKILL)

Real example:

Jenkins job stuck
ECS task hung
Zombie child processes not clearing

Interview line:

“If a process doesn’t terminate gracefully, I use SIGKILL (-9) to forcefully stop it.”


Zombie Process (Important interview point)

Scenario:
You see process like:

ps aux | grep Z

Why it happens:

Parent didn’t collect child process exit status

Fix:

Restart parent process
or
Kill parent → system cleans zombie

Interview line:

“Zombie processes occur when child processes exit but parent doesn’t read their status. I identify them using ps and resolve by restarting or killing the parent process.”


Foreground vs Background

Scenario:
Running long script or deployment

Commands:

./deploy.sh &     # Run in background
jobs              # Check jobs
fg %1             # Bring to foreground

Real example:

Running long Terraform apply
Background log monitoring

In real-time, I use ps aux for quick snapshots to identify processes, and top/htop for live monitoring during CPU or memory spikes. If a process is unresponsive, I first try graceful termination using kill, and if that fails, I use kill -9 to force stop it. I’ve also handled zombie processes by identifying them using ps and resolving by restarting or killing the parent process. Additionally, I use background and foreground job control when running long tasks like deployments.”


. df -h – Filesystem usage

Scenario:
Production server alerts → “Disk almost full (90%+)”

What you do:

df -h

Why:

Shows overall filesystem usage (/, /var, /home, etc.)
Helps identify which partition is full

Real example:

/var partition full → logs consuming space → application crashes

Interview line:

“I use df -h to quickly check filesystem usage and identify which partition is running out of space.”

✅ 2. du -sh * – Directory-level analysis

Scenario:
You found /var is full → now need to find what exactly is filling it

What you do:

cd /var
du -sh *

Why:

Shows size of each directory
Helps pinpoint heavy folders (logs, cache, backups)

Deep dive (real troubleshooting):

du -sh /var/log/*

Real example:

Log files (like /var/log/nginx or app logs) growing huge
Old backups not cleaned

Interview line:

“After identifying a full filesystem using df, I use du to drill down and find which directories are consuming space.”

free -m – Memory usage

Scenario:
Application is slow / server lagging / alerts for high memory usage

What you do:

free -m

Why:

Shows total, used, free memory
Shows swap usage (very important in troubleshooting)

Key understanding:

Low free memory + high swap = performance issue

Real example:

Java app consuming too much RAM → system starts swapping → slow API

Interview line:

“I use free -m to check RAM and swap usage. High swap usage usually indicates memory pressure affecting performance.”

